WEBVTT

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:06.040
Hi, this is Dr. Jelena Trajkovic from the CECS Department at Cal State Long Beach.

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:12.040
Let us now learn how to code registers in Verilog.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:18.040
And we are going to start this with the example of basic registers.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:24.040
So, as in our lab, our example is 8-bit basic registers.

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:29.040
Its inputs are clock and reset at the input,

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:34.600
so it's 8-bit register, so DIN is from seven to zero,

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:39.900
and output is Q, which is of a register type, seven to zero.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:47.060
Code for register looks pretty simple.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.580
It just, for basic register,

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:52.340
it just has several lines of code.

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:56.780
However, it is very, very important to get

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:02.740
all the components that go into this to some extent

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:07.700
simple looking, but not very simple code to start with.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:11.780
So first of all, inside of always statement,

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:14.260
inside its sensitivity list,

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:17.140
we have only clock and reset.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:21.160
And specifically, we are going to,

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:27.730
our register is going to be reevaluated

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:33.570
or updated only on positive edge of reset

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:35.010
and positive edge of clock.

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:38.530
So basically, that means only when either

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:43.620
of those signals change from zero to one.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:45.160
Reset has priority.

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:49.240
That means when we hit reset button on our FPGA board

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:53.740
or specify reset equals to one in our test bench,

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:58.540
We do not need to know what other signals are.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:03.540
The content of the register needs to be placed to zero.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:07.620
So that is why first thing in this always block,

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:12.260
we ask if reset, basically if reset equals to one.

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:17.260
And in that case, we update value of Q to all zeros.

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:22.660
This is hexadecimal, so eight bits represented

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:25.640
and hexadecimal are zero zero.

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:30.300
Otherwise, if reset is equal to zero

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:34.220
and only off on positive edge of the clock,

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:37.820
so that is what else part of the statement describes,

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:42.820
Q becomes Dn, basically the value that is on data input

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:48.860
gets to be stored on Q.

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:52.100
Other than that, on all other times,

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:56.980
if you keeps its value, preserves the old value.

00:02:56.980 --> 00:03:01.980
Please be mindful that in the remaining portions of labs

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:06.980
you will have the in load count enable

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:11.480
and you might have for any other multifunctional registers,

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:13.500
different other signals.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:17.700
None of them make it on the sensitivity list.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.820
Only thing that makes into sensitivity list

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:24.660
and that is a rule for sequential circuits,

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:27.220
our clock and reset,

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:29.220
and in our particular case,

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:32.580
we are going to use them on positive edge.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:37.020
Should we have any deviation of that, I will warn you.

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:38.360
This is a general rule.

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:42.800
Also some hints for the remaining portion

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:44.100
of our lab assignment.

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:48.380
First thing first, how do we code for log register?

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:59.020
This register, as we saw in our examples that we did on Blackboard during the class time,

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:04.120
has load signal that is going to enable or disable loading here.

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:13.260
So inside of if statement, we're going to use basically LD signal to determine if we

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:16.180
we are going to update the register.

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:19.340
And you need to fill in the body of the SIF statement

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:20.320
with your code.

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:25.380
You do not need to write else portion of the code

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:28.100
for this particular SIF statement.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:31.540
It would be Q equals to Q,

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:35.500
which means if you would keep it, preserve its old value.

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:39.900
However, that is implicitly always happening.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:44.900
So value of the variable that has type of register

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:52.880
will be preserved unless it is updated again.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:57.000
Another hint for the lab that I hope you will find useful

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:58.800
is that for the counter,

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:01.760
you will need to constantly increment

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:05.540
all positive edge of the clock

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:09.400
and incrementing can be simply done

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:12.160
by assignment statement,

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:15.040
which is going to be placed in the proper part

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:19.080
of your always statement in your lab.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:22.640
So Q is going to get value of Q plus one

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:26.280
and that is basically simply it.

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:29.080
That is how your counter is going to count.

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:31.200
I hope this was helpful.

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:32.400
Thank you for watching.

